+//! What this service is doing, in numbers a scraper can read.
+//!
+//! `mailer/1.20-metrics` put a `/metrics` endpoint on a service and counted requests with it. That
+//! answered "how much traffic" and nothing else. The three numbers that answer "is delivery
+//! healthy, and for whom" are different, and each one has a shape of its own:
+//!
+//! * **how long an attempt takes, as a distribution, per tenant.** An average hides the only thing
+//!   anybody acts on: the tail. A p99 needs buckets, and the buckets are a histogram — which is
+//!   also the only metric here whose *rising* value is a promise about something that has not
+//!   happened yet;
+//! * **how much work is still owed, per tenant.** A latency without a backlog tells you something
+//!   is slow and not whether it is falling behind. The gauge is read from the queue when it is
+//!   scraped rather than remembered between turns: "what do we owe right now" answered from a
+//!   cache is a number about the past;
+//! * **what attempts came to, per tenant.** Counted once per attempt, by the outcome the worker
+//!   decided on, so `sent` and `dead` count deliveries that ended and `retried` counts the attempts
+//!   that were handed back. A total that only counted successes cannot show a spike in failures at
+//!   all.
+//!
+//! ## The label set is a budget, not a description
+//!
+//! Every distinct label value is a series, a series costs memory in every scraper and index in the
+//! store behind it, and the cardinality of a metric is the product of its labels' values. So:
+//!
+//! * **the tenant label carries the tenant's *name*** — at most thirty-two characters of a closed
+//!   alphabet, one per row in `tenants`, and the set of them is bounded by configuration rather
+//!   than by traffic;
+//! * **nothing else is a label.** Not the endpoint's URL (a tenant may register thousands), not the
+//!   event id, not the delivery id, not the URL's path. Those belong in a log line, where they cost
+//!   a line rather than a series, and the trap of this step is a service whose metrics endpoint
+//!   grows until the scrape times out;
+//! * **a value outside the known set folds into `unknown`.** A label value this service did not
+//!   choose is a value somebody else did, and a metric that reports whatever it is handed has a
+//!   cardinality nobody can bound.
+//!
+//! ## What is deliberately not here
+//!
+//! No tracing, no histograms of queue depth, and no per-route metrics: the HTTP surface of this
+//! service is four routes and one tenant-scoped shape, and `relay/2.16-authz-audit` already writes
+//! down who did what. A metrics module that measures everything measures nothing anybody reads —
+//! and the two numbers an operator actually pages on, the backlog and the tail latency, are the two
+//! this module exists for.
+//!
+//! The text [`Metrics::render`] produces is the Prometheus exposition format: one `# HELP` and one
+//! `# TYPE` line per family, then one line per sample, `name{label="value"} value`. It is written
+//! by hand for the reason the rest of this project is: what a scraper reads should be visible in
+//! the code that produces it.
+
+use std::sync::Mutex;
+use std::time::Duration;
+
+use sqlx::PgPool;
+
+use crate::error::Error;
+
+/// The label value everything that is not a tenant name folds into.
+pub const UNKNOWN: &str = "unknown";
+
+/// The upper bound of each histogram bucket, in seconds.
+///
+/// The bounds are the ones a webhook attempt lives inside: a healthy subscriber answers in tens of
+/// milliseconds, a slow one in seconds, and anything past ten seconds is `Policy::deadline`'s
+/// business rather than a distribution's. They are fixed rather than configurable, because a bucket
+/// that moves between deploys is a dashboard that moves with it.
+pub const BUCKETS: [f64; 11] = [
+    0.005, 0.01, 0.025, 0.05, 0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 1.0, 2.5, 5.0, 10.0,
+];
+
+/// What an attempt came to, as far as the traffic is concerned.
+///
+/// Three values and no more: this is a *label*, and a fourth value for "we could not record it" or
+/// "we did not try" would be a series that means "ask the log". The two outcomes the worker can also
+/// report — an attempt it could not write down (`lost`) and one it never made because the
+/// endpoint's breaker is open (`shed`) — are facts about the worker, and they stay in its report.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord)]
+pub enum Outcome {
+    /// The subscriber took it.
+    Sent,
+    /// The attempt failed and the delivery is owed again.
+    Retried,
+    /// The last attempt this delivery was going to get failed.
+    Dead,
+}
+
+/// Every outcome, for the tests that walk the label's values.
+pub const OUTCOMES: [Outcome; 3] = [Outcome::Sent, Outcome::Retried, Outcome::Dead];
+
+impl Outcome {
+    /// The outcome as text: the label's value, and a stable token a query groups by.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn as_str(self) -> &'static str {
+        match self {
+            Self::Sent => "sent",
+            Self::Retried => "retried",
+            Self::Dead => "dead",
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// The numbers one process exports.
+///
+/// `record` takes `&self` because the attempts that record are several tasks of one turn running at
+/// once: a registry handed out as `&mut` would serialise the deliveries it measures, which is a
+/// measurement that changes the thing it measures. Whatever keeps the observations inside is
+/// therefore behind a lock or an atomic, and it is the scaffold's job to say *that* rather than
+/// which one.
+#[derive(Debug, Default)]
+pub struct Metrics {
+    /// Everything observed so far. Its shape is yours; what has to be true of it is that `record`
+    /// takes `&self` and that a histogram is kept per tenant label.
+    state: Mutex<State>,
+}
+
+/// What one process has observed.
+///
+/// Fill it in with what the two methods below need: an observation per tenant label, and a count
+/// per label and outcome.
+#[derive(Debug, Default)]
+struct State {}
+
+impl Metrics {
+    /// A registry with nothing in it.
+    #[must_use]
